{"name":"Isolation","hex":"#494d55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"isolation","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state of being isolated, detached, or separated; the state of being away from other people.","The act of isolating."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/isolation"}],"entries":[{"title":"Social isolation","blocks":[{"text":"Social isolation is a state of complete or near-complete lack of contact between an individual and society. It differs from loneliness, which reflects temporary and involuntary lack of contact with other humans in the world. Social isolation can be an issue for individuals of any age, though symptoms may differ by age group."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_isolation","wikidata":"Q1778765"},{"title":"Isolation (psychology)","blocks":[{"text":"Isolation is a defence mechanism in psychoanalytic theory, first proposed by Sigmund Freud. While related to repression, the concept distinguishes itself in several ways. It is characterized as a mental process involving the creation of a gap between an unpleasant or threatening cognition and other thoughts and feelings. By minimizing associative connections with other thoughts, the threatening cognition is remembered less often and is less likely to affect self-esteem or self concept. \nFreud illustrated the concept with the example of a person beginning a train of thought and then pausing for a moment before continuing to a different subject. His theory stated that by inserting an interval, the person was \"letting it be understood symbolically that he will not allow his thoughts about that impression or activity to come into associative contact with other thoughts.\" As a defense against harmful thoughts, isolation prevents the self from allowing these cognitions to become recurrent and possibly damaging to the self-concept."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isolation_(psychology)","wikidata":"Q312714"},{"title":"Isolation (database systems)","blocks":[{"text":"In database systems, isolation is one of the ACID transaction properties. It determines how transaction integrity is visible to other users and systems. A lower isolation level increases the ability of many users to access the same data at the same time, but also increases the number of concurrency effects users might encounter. Conversely, a higher isolation level reduces the types of concurrency effects that users may encounter, but requires more system resources and increases the chances that one transaction will block another."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isolation_(database_systems)","wikidata":"Q1674485"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"isolation":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Berrely-isolation.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/18/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Berrely-isolation.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Berrely-isolation.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/18/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Berrely-isolation.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Berrely-isolation.wav.ogg"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-isolation.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/9a/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-isolation.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-isolation.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/9a/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-isolation.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-isolation.wav.ogg"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/29/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Wodencafe-isolation.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&ut